Make nvtt's isValidPtr use the 64-bit branch on every 64-bit target.

bimg vendors nvtt, whose isValidPtr() picks its pointer-sanity check with
`#if NV_CPU_X86_64 || NV_CPU_AARCH64`. Both arms are wrong for any other
64-bit architecture: the fallback casts a pointer to uint32, which GCC
rejects outright ("cast from 'const void*' to 'uint32' loses precision
[-fpermissive]") and which would silently compare truncated addresses if it
did compile. The riscv64 cross lane hits this; so would ppc64le, s390x,
mips64 or loongarch64.

Gate on pointer width instead of an architecture allow-list, so the check is
correct on 64-bit targets nvtt has never heard of and unchanged on 32-bit
ones. NV_CPU_X86_64 / NV_CPU_AARCH64 are kept in the condition so the branch
is still taken on those two even if the compiler defines neither width macro.

--- a/bimg/3rdparty/nvtt/nvcore/debug.h
+++ b/bimg/3rdparty/nvtt/nvcore/debug.h
@@ -166,7 +166,7 @@ NVCORE_API void NV_CDECL nvDebugPrint( const char *msg, ... ) __attribute__((for
 namespace nv
 {
     inline bool isValidPtr(const void * ptr) {
-    #if NV_CPU_X86_64 || NV_CPU_AARCH64
+    #if NV_CPU_X86_64 || NV_CPU_AARCH64 || defined(__LP64__) || defined(_LP64) || defined(_WIN64) || (defined(__SIZEOF_POINTER__) && __SIZEOF_POINTER__ == 8)
         if (ptr == NULL) return true;
         if (reinterpret_cast<uint64>(ptr) < 0x10000ULL) return false;
         if (reinterpret_cast<uint64>(ptr) >= 0x000007FFFFFEFFFFULL) return false;
